From 34b9a63d5c0a9ecbfdcd3517f2962d63efb819e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 21:40:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 094/145] media: meson: vdec: vp9: do not take vp9->lock
 recursively on a source change

codec_vp9_threaded_isr() holds vp9->lock across its whole body, and calls
amvdec_src_change() from inside it.  When the capture queue already suits
the new format - same width, same height, enough buffers, still streaming -
amvdec_src_change() does not queue an event and wait for userspace; it
calls sess->fmt_out->codec_ops->resume(sess) directly.  For VP9 that is
codec_vp9_resume(), whose first act is mutex_lock(&vp9->lock).

The IRQ thread therefore blocks on a mutex it already owns and never
returns.  Every further decode stalls behind it:

  INFO: task irq/33-vdec:514 blocked for more than 30 seconds.
    __mutex_lock_slowpath
    codec_vp9_resume+0x1c/0x88 [meson_vdec]
    amvdec_src_change+0x110/0x120 [meson_vdec]
    codec_vp9_threaded_isr+0x1c8/0xddc [meson_vdec]
    vdec_threaded_isr+0x1c/0x24 [meson_vdec]
  INFO: task irq/33-vdec:514 is blocked on a mutex likely owned by
        task irq/33-vdec:514.

It is easy to hit in practice: seeking within a VP9 stream makes the
parser re-announce the frame geometry, and on a player that has already
negotiated that same geometry every condition of the fast path is met.
Observed on a Khadas VIM3 (G12B) seeking a 4K VP9 profile-2 clip; the
decoder never recovers and the board needs a reboot.

Drop the lock before calling amvdec_src_change().  The pending frame is
shown and the references saved first, so nothing the lock protects is
still live at that point, and the handler returns immediately afterwards
instead of falling through to the shared unlock.

codec_hevc_resume() and codec_h264_resume() do not take a codec lock, so
VP9 is the only affected decoder.

Fixes: 00c43088aa68 ("media: meson: vdec: add VP9 decoder support")
Signed-off-by: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
---
 drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_vp9.c | 16 ++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_vp9.c b/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_vp9.c
index f63bbdcb5814..d69e338e3a2b 100644
--- a/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_vp9.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_vp9.c
@@ -2140,9 +2140,6 @@ static irqreturn_t codec_vp9_threaded_isr(struct amvdec_session *sess)
 
 	codec_vp9_fetch_rpm(sess);
 	if (codec_vp9_process_rpm(vp9)) {
-		amvdec_src_change(sess, vp9->width, vp9->height, 16,
-				  vp9->is_10bit ? 10 : 8);
-
 		/* No frame is actually processed */
 		vp9->cur_frame = NULL;
 
@@ -2153,7 +2150,18 @@ static irqreturn_t codec_vp9_threaded_isr(struct amvdec_session *sess)
 		if (vp9->frames_num)
 			codec_vp9_save_refs(vp9);
 
-		goto unlock;
+		/*
+		 * amvdec_src_change() calls ->resume() directly when the
+		 * capture queue already suits the new format, and
+		 * codec_vp9_resume() takes vp9->lock - which this thread is
+		 * holding.  Drop it first: the pending frame has been shown
+		 * and the refs saved, so there is nothing left here that the
+		 * lock protects.
+		 */
+		mutex_unlock(&vp9->lock);
+		amvdec_src_change(sess, vp9->width, vp9->height, 16,
+				  vp9->is_10bit ? 10 : 8);
+		return IRQ_HANDLED;
 	}
 
 	codec_vp9_process_lf(vp9);
-- 
